<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document"> Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Aya E. Fouda<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:fouda@compumacy.com">fouda@compumacy.com</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">A. A. Hassan<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Radwa J. Hanafy
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mohammed E. Fouda<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">†</sup><sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup">†</sup>Compumacy for Artificial Intelligence solutions, Cairo, Egypt.<sup id="id5" class="ltx_sup">‡</sup>Department of Behavioural Health- Saint Elizabeths Hospital, Washington DC, 20032
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) hold great promise in enhancing psychiatric practice, from improving diagnostic accuracy to streamlining clinical documentation and therapeutic support. However, existing evaluation resources heavily rely on small clinical interview corpora, social media posts, or synthetic dialogues, which limits their clinical validity and fails to capture the full complexity of psychiatric reasoning. In this work, we introduce PsychiatryBench, a rigorously curated benchmark grounded exclusively in authoritative, expert-validated psychiatric textbooks and casebooks (e.g., DSM-5-TR Clinical Cases, Stahl’s Essential Psychopharmacology). PsychiatryBench comprises eleven distinct question-answering tasks ranging from diagnostic reasoning and treatment planning to longitudinal follow-up, management planning, clinical approach, sequential case analysis, and multiple-choice/extended matching formats totaling over 5,300 expert-annotated items. We evaluate a diverse set of frontier LLMs (including Google Gemini, DeepSeek, LLaMA 3, and QWQ-32) alongside leading open-source medical models (e.g., OpenBiloLLM, MedGemma) using both conventional metrics and an "LLM-as-judge" similarity scoring framework. Our results reveal substantial gaps in clinical consistency and safety, particularly in multi-turn follow-up and management tasks, underscoring the need for specialized model tuning and more robust evaluation paradigms. PsychiatryBench offers a modular, extensible platform for benchmarking and improving LLM performance in high-stakes mental health applications.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id6" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">K</em><span id="p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">eywords</span> PsychiatryBench  <math id="p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Large Language Models  <math id="p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Psychiatric Benchmarking  <math id="p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Diagnostic evaluation  <math id="p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
Mental Illness</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs), a subset of artificial intelligence (AI), have the potential to completely transform the way mental illness is identified, diagnosed, and treated. In 2019, mental disorders accounted for 5.1% of the world’s disease burden, with over 280 million people suffering from depressive disorders and around 703,000 people dying from suicide <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Between 2024 and 2040, the total economic burden of mental health problems in the United States is expected to reach around $14 trillion, including direct medical expenses, emergency room visits, lost productivity, and premature death <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">New avenues for mental health research were made possible with the introduction of Natural Language Processing (NLP). Early applications focused on analyzing text from self-report questionnaires and clinical interviews using techniques like the Linguistic Inquiry and Word Count (LIWC) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. A significant advancement came with transformer-based models such as BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, which learn complex linguistic representations from large text corpora without manual feature engineering. These models have shown strong performance in tasks like sentiment analysis, suicide risk assessment, and detecting mental health disorders from social media content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, despite these advances, there remains a critical gap: existing evaluation frameworks rarely test models on tasks that reflect the depth and complexity of real-world psychiatric decision-making. Most benchmarks rely on simplified labels or non-clinical data, limiting their relevance to actual clinical practice. To address this, our study introduces a new benchmark grounded in expert-curated psychiatric sources and designed to assess nuanced reasoning tasks such as differential diagnosis and treatment planning. This framework not only raises the bar for clinical relevance in LLM evaluation but also enables robust comparison across state-of-the-art models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The integration of LLMs into mental healthcare represents a significant technological shift, with the potential to transform clinical practice across the entire psychiatric workflow<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. In diagnostics, LLMs demonstrate effectiveness in detecting disorders like depression from textual data; in therapeutic interventions, through conversational agents; and in clinical documentation, their automated notes have been preferred by therapists in blind tests over human-written ones<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Intriguingly, some studies suggest that LLMs can even surpass human physicians in expressing empathy in written communication<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. This transformative potential, however, is counterbalanced by significant risks that necessitate an emerging field that can be termed ’Evaluation Psychiatry.’ A primary concern is the propensity of LLMs to generate inaccurate or nonsensical responses, known as "hallucinations," which raises substantial safety concerns in a clinical domain where errors can have profound consequences pandey2024harnessinglargelanguagemodels. This tension between the promise of democratizing mental health access and the ethical imperative of patient safety underscores the critical need for rigorous, clinically-grounded evaluation frameworks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The root of this tension can be traced to the evolution of the datasets used to evaluate these models. Early, influential resources like the Distress Analysis Interview Corpus (DAIC-WOZ) provided clinically relevant transcripts that enabled foundational methods, but these datasets were small and faced challenges in generalizability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. To achieve greater scale, a second wave of datasets leveraged social media and public surveys, such as the subreddit Mental Health Posts dataset<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Models like MentalBERT, pretrained on millions of sentences from mental health subreddits, achieved high accuracy (over 94%) on simplified tasks like binary depression classification<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. While valuable for demonstrating potential, this approach has a critical flaw: its reliance on unverified, non-clinical data oversimplifies complex psychiatric disorders and risks reflecting trends in self-diagnosis rather than formal clinical presentations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, the field has begun to address this by developing more complex, conversational benchmarks. MentalChat16K, for example, combines synthetic data with anonymized clinical transcripts to evaluate an LLM’s ability to engage in nuanced dialogue<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Reflects a maturation of the field, moving beyond simple classification. Nonetheless, this third wave introduces new methodological challenges. The reliance on synthetic data risks creating benchmarks that do not capture the full complexity of real human interaction. At the same time, the use of LLM-as-a-judge evaluation methods introduces a circular dependency, where the technology being assessed is also the tool for its assessment, potentially masking its own biases and unreliability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This analysis of the current landscape reveals a critical and persistent gap: the lack of a comprehensive benchmark that is simultaneously grounded in authoritative, expert-validated knowledge and designed to evaluate the full spectrum of complex clinical reasoning. There is a stark contrast between the datasets currently in use and the foundational knowledge sources that form the bedrock of clinical psychiatric practice. Authoritative textbooks and casebooks, such as the <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DSM-5-TR Clinical Cases<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.p7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a><span id="S1.p7.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">]</span></cite></span> and <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stahl’s Essential Psychopharmacology<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S1.p7.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">[</span><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a><span id="S1.p7.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">]</span></cite></span>, represent knowledge curated and validated by experts over decades. These resources, which illustrate the complexities of differential diagnosis and treatment planning, stand as the gold standard against which an LLM’s clinical acumen and safety must ultimately be measured.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The present study is positioned to address these limitations directly. We introduce a novel evaluation framework meticulously constructed from the foundational psychiatric textbooks described above, deliberately prioritizing clinical accuracy, safety, and alignment with established medical practice. Our contribution is threefold:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.25in;">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We construct a novel, diverse dataset curated from authoritative clinical casebooks, diagnostic manuals, and specialized guides, ensuring that our evaluation is grounded in validated, expert knowledge rather than unverified or synthetic data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design eleven distinct and complex QA task types, ranging from diagnostic reasoning and treatment planning to sequential, case-based analysis, to create a framework that evaluates the sophisticated cognitive functions required in real-world psychiatry, moving far beyond simple classification.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We deploy this framework to conduct a rigorous evaluation of leading frontier models, establishing a crucial benchmark that provides novel insights into the current capabilities and inherent risks of LLMs in a clinical context.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A comparative evaluation of several state-of-the-art LLMs, including Gemini 2.5 Pro, Gemini 2.5 flash, Claude Opus, and Qwen/QWQ-32, selected for their strong performance across reasoning and safety benchmarks, and for their relevance in current clinical LLM discourse.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S1.p8.2" class="ltx_p">This work, therefore, moves beyond testing what LLMs can do on simplified tasks and begins to assess their readiness for the complex, rigorous, high-stakes reality of clinical mental healthcare. The remainder of the paper is structured as follows: Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Related Work ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> provides a detailed review of LLM applications in mental health and the landscape of psychiatric datasets. Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Datasets ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> describes the design, sourcing, and annotation of our novel psychiatric QA dataset. Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experimental Setup ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> outlines the experimental framework, and Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Methodology Implementation ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> details the implementation. Section <a href="#S6" title="6 Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> presents and interprets our results. Section <a href="#S7" title="7 Limitations and Future Work ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> discusses study limitations and future work, and Section <a href="#S8" title="8 Conclusion ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> concludes the paper by summarizing our key contributions. Finally, Ethical, Copyright, and Fair Use Statements are presented.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we review the evolving landscape of LLMs in psychiatry and mental health, focusing on both their application potential and the underlying evaluation frameworks. We begin by surveying clinical uses of LLMs, ranging from diagnostics and therapy to documentation, and examining the ethical and safety concerns arising from their deployment. We then analyze the major datasets that have shaped model development in this space, highlighting limitations in clinical grounding, generalization, and task complexity. Finally, we discuss the importance of using expert-validated knowledge sources, such as psychiatric casebooks and diagnostic manuals, to develop robust, clinically meaningful benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>The Emergence of LLMs in Mental Healthcare</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The integration of LLMs into healthcare represents a significant technological shift, with the potential to transform clinical practice, particularly in mental health <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. The field is witnessing a rapid expansion of LLM applications across the entire psychiatric workflow. In diagnostics, LLMs demonstrate effectiveness in detecting mental health disorders and suicidal ideation from textual data, often sourced from social media, and they can translate complex diagnostic manuals like the ICD-11 into inspectable logic programs for expert review <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. For therapeutic interventions, conversational agents such as Woebot and Wysa, built on principles of cognitive behavioral therapy, are providing accessible and de-stigmatized eHealth services. Intriguingly, some studies suggest that LLMs can even surpass human physicians in expressing empathy in written communication <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, in clinical documentation, LLMs offer dramatic efficiency gains by automating the generation of medical notes. In this task, therapists have preferred their output in blind tests over human-written notes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This transformative potential, however, is counterbalanced by significant risks that necessitate an emerging field that can be termed ’Evaluation Psychiatry.’ A primary concern is the propensity of LLMs to generate inaccurate or nonsensical responses, known as "hallucinations," which raises substantial safety concerns in a clinical domain where errors can have profound consequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. This risk mandates stringent ethical guidelines and a "human-in-the-loop" paradigm where AI outputs are subject to expert validation. Clinicians also express ambivalence, worrying about over-reliance on "black box" algorithms, the erosion of clinical autonomy, and the potential for training data to embed biases related to socioeconomic status or geography, which could exacerbate healthcare disparities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. This tension between the promise of democratizing mental health access and the ethical imperative of patient safety underscores the critical need for rigorous, clinically-grounded evaluation frameworks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>The Landscape of Datasets for Psychiatric Evaluation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The evolution of LLM evaluation in mental health can be traced through the datasets used to train and test them. Early and influential resources include clinical interview corpora like the Distress Analysis Interview Corpus (DAIC-WOZ) and its extension, E-DAIC.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> These datasets, containing transcripts of semi-structured interviews and corresponding psychiatric assessment scores (e.g., PHQ-8 for depression), have been instrumental. They enabled innovative methods such as the Language Model for Impersonation-based Questionnaire Completion (LMIQ) framework, which attempts to bridge unstructured patient narratives with quantitative metrics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. While a significant step, these methods face challenges in generalization beyond their specific benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A second wave of datasets has leveraged the scale of social media and public surveys. Resources like the Reddit Mental Health Posts dataset, the Depression Detection Using Text corpus, and various mental health surveys have provided vast amounts of natural language data for tasks like sentiment analysis and binary classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Models like Mental BERT and Mental RoBERTa were pretrained on millions of sentences from mental health-related subreddits to improve performance in these tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. However, the clinical validity of social media data is a significant concern due to its unverified nature, oversimplification of symptoms, and the risk of reflecting trends in self-diagnosis rather than formal clinical presentations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, the field has begun to address the need for more complex, conversational benchmarks. MentalChat16K, for instance, is a benchmark for conversational mental health assistance that combines synthetic data with anonymized clinical transcripts. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. Simultaneously, researchers have also investigated automated approaches for enhancing dataset richness and annotation quality. For example, Hassan et al.(2024) introduced a synthetic labeling framework that transforms traditional single<span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>disorder datasets into multi<span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>label corpora by leveraging zero<span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>shot and prompt<span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text">-</span>driven LLM annotation strategies.
Their method, exemplified by the SPAADE<span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text">-</span>DR dataset, better captures co<span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text">-</span>occurring disorders such as depression, anxiety, PTSD, ADHD, and eating disorders, thus enabling more nuanced diagnostic modeling<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. This reflects a maturation of the field, moving beyond simple classification towards evaluating LLMs on their ability to engage in nuanced, therapeutic dialogue. Nonetheless, the reliance on synthetic data and LLM-as-a-judge evaluation methods introduces new layers of potential bias and unreliability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Foundational Knowledge Sources in Psychiatric Practice</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure clinical relevance and safety, any robust evaluation of LLMs in psychiatry must be grounded in the authoritative knowledge that defines the field. The gold standard for this knowledge is found in established psychiatric textbooks and clinical guides, which provide the basis for professional training and practice. The following sources represent the bedrock of clinical knowledge that LLMs must be tested against:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.25in;">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diagnostic Manuals and Casebooks:</span> Books like <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DSM-5 Clinical Cases</span> and its successor, <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DSM-5-TR Clinical Cases</span>, are essential companions to the official diagnostic manual. They bring diagnostic criteria to life through realistic narratives and detailed clinical discussions, illustrating the complexities of comorbidity and differential diagnosis. Similarly, texts such as <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">100 Cases in Psychiatry</span> and <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Case Files Psychiatry</span> provide high-yield clinical scenarios designed to sharpen practical decision-making skills, making them ideal for evaluating an LLM’s clinical reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Specialized Clinical Guides:</span> Deep domain expertise is captured in specialized resources. <span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stahl’s Essential Psychopharmacology</span> and its case-based companions are seminal texts on the neurobiological basis and practical application of psychiatric medications, crucial for evaluating treatment-related tasks. Likewise, <span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Geriatric Psychiatry</span> provides comprehensive knowledge on the unique diagnostic and treatment considerations for elderly populations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structured Knowledge and Assessment Tools:</span> To test factual recall and application of diagnostic criteria in a standardized format, resources like <span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">DSM-5-TR Self-Exam Questions</span> and <span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">MCQs in Psychology</span> are invaluable. They provide thousands of multiple-choice questions that mirror the format of professional board examinations, offering a structured way to measure an LLM’s knowledge base.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These foundational texts, curated and validated by experts over decades, stand in stark contrast to the unverified and often transient nature of data from social media. Grounding an evaluation dataset in these sources is a deliberate choice to prioritize clinical accuracy, safety, and alignment with established medical practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Synthesis of Prior Work and Remaining Gaps</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In reviewing the landscape, it is clear that prior work has made significant strides. LLMs have proven highly effective at classification tasks, such as achieving a balanced accuracy of 94.8% for depression detection and 96.2% for PTSD when incorporated via decision-level fusion in multimodal systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>. They have also demonstrated utility in administrative roles and have shown surprising capabilities in generating empathetic responses. These successes, however, also illuminate what is missing. The field’s reliance on datasets derived from social media, while useful for scale, lacks the clinical rigor required for high-stakes applications. Evaluation has often been limited to simple classification or sentiment analysis, failing to assess the complex, multi-step reasoning that defines clinical practice. Even with more advanced methods like LMIQ, challenges of generalization and the risk of hallucination persist.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This analysis reveals a critical gap in the literature: a comprehensive benchmark that is simultaneously grounded in authoritative, expert-validated knowledge and designed to evaluate the full spectrum of complex clinical reasoning. There is an urgent need to move beyond binary classification and simple QA to assess an LLM’s ability to perform tasks that require nuanced understanding, such as formulating a differential diagnosis, creating a multi-stage management plan, or engaging in sequential, case-based reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The present study is positioned to address these limitations directly. By constructing a novel dataset meticulously curated from the foundational psychiatric textbooks described above, we ensure clinical validity and accuracy. By designing eleven distinct and complex QA task types ranging from diagnostic reasoning to treatment planning and sequential analysis, we create a framework to evaluate the sophisticated cognitive functions required in psychiatry. This work, therefore, moves beyond testing what LLMs can do on simplified tasks and begins to assess their readiness for the complex, rigorous, high-stakes reality of clinical mental healthcare.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.5 </span>Benchmarks for Evaluating LLMs in Psychiatry and Mental Health</h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, two notable benchmarks illustrate different approaches for mental health and psychiatry. From Empathy to Action: Benchmarking LLMs in Mental Health with MentalBench-10 and a Novel Cognitive-Affective Evaluation Approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> proposed a framework that emphasizes empathy, affective alignment, and the capacity of LLMs to translate understanding into actionable guidance. MentalBench-10 focuses on cognitive-affective evaluations, combining both synthetic and user-generated dialogues to assess empathy, appropriateness of advice, and general mental health support. While this contributes valuable insights into the emotional and supportive dimensions of LLM performance, it primarily emphasizes conversational empathy and cognitive-affective alignment rather than the depth of psychiatric reasoning required for clinical tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly, PsychBench: A comprehensive and professional benchmark for evaluating the performance of LLM-assisted psychiatric clinical practice <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> introduced a wide-ranging evaluation platform that incorporates tasks from diagnosis and treatment to professional communication. Its evaluation metrics include ICD Primary Diagnosis Accuracy, Top Choice Alignment Score (TCAS), Medication Match Score (MMS), and Recommendation Coverage Rate (RCR). Although PsychBench benefits from real-world, diverse clinical cases and validation by psychiatrists, its relatively small dataset (300 cases) restricts generalization, and it still relies partly on synthetic dialogue construction, with limited grounding in authoritative, expert-validated psychiatric sources.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, PsychiatryBench is curated from authoritative psychiatric textbooks and casebooks, aiming to provide comprehensive psychiatric reasoning and knowledge evaluation. It introduces eleven distinct QA tasks including diagnostic reasoning, treatment planning, sequential case analysis, and longitudinal follow-up covering more than 5,300 expert-annotated items. The benchmark relies on LLM-as-judge similarity scoring but is built on an expert-validated, broad knowledge base. By grounding its dataset exclusively in clinically validated sources (e.g., DSM-5-TR Clinical Cases, Stahl’s Essential Psychopharmacology), PsychiatryBench directly addresses the gaps left by prior efforts. In contrast to MentalBench-10, which emphasizes affective alignment, and PsychBench, which provides broad but synthetic coverage, our work offers a benchmark that is simultaneously clinically rigorous, multi-task, and extensible.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Datasets</h2>

<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.09711v1/History_16.png" id="S3.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/468;" width="476" height="468" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Overview of the full PsychiatryBench dataset, including manual extraction and filtering steps.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.09711v1/History_13.png" id="S3.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/537;" width="476" height="537" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Structure of the selected subset (S-dataset) used for LLM evaluation.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Visual representation of the PsychiatryBench dataset development pipeline and its final evaluation subset.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The data set used in this study was manually curated to address the unique challenges of psychiatric question answering (QA), with a focus on a diverse range of clinically relevant tasks. Sourced from authoritative psychiatry textbooks and expert-validated clinical resources, the dataset comprises natural language questions paired with expert-formulated answers. These questions are grounded in real-world psychiatric scenarios. They are designed to evaluate the reasoning, decision-making, and knowledge application abilities of LLMs in both clinical and educational contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Datasets ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> provides a visual summary of the composition across eleven task categories, while Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 Datasets ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> outlines the quantitative distribution of samples per task type. Together, they offer a comprehensive overview of the dataset structure and task diversity used in model evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The manual collection process ensured that the dataset maintains high-quality, medically accurate content, aligning with standardized diagnostic frameworks such as the DSM-5 and ICD-10, as well as clinical practice guidelines from organizations like NICE and the American Psychiatric Association (APA).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The dataset is structured around eleven distinct QA task types, each representing a different cognitive or clinical function critical to psychiatric practice:</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.25in;">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diagnosis:</span> This subset contains 467 expert-annotated question-answer pairs centered on diagnostic reasoning. Each item includes a clinical vignette and a diagnostic query, requiring inference of the most appropriate psychiatric diagnosis. The dataset represents 123 unique diagnostic questions and 295 distinct clinical histories. Examples are drawn from six authoritative sources, including 100 Cases in Psychiatry, Geriatric Psychiatry, and Case Files Psychiatry.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Treatment:</span>
This subset comprises 258 expert-annotated clinical question-answer pairs focused on treatment decision-making in psychiatry. Each example includes a detailed case history, a mental state examination, and a treatment-related clinical question, accompanied by a model answer curated from authoritative psychiatry textbooks. The scenarios reflect real-world psychiatric care and cover a wide range of disorders and treatment modalities, including pharmacological management, psychological therapies (e.g., CBT, psychodynamic therapy), and patient-specific treatment considerations. Most cases are sourced from well-established educational resources such as <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">100 Cases in Psychiatry</span>, <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Case Files Psychiatry</span>, and <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Geriatric Psychiatry</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Treatment Follow-Up:</span>
This dataset comprises 27 clinical question-answer pairs focused on the follow-up phase of psychiatric treatment. Each scenario includes a psychiatric case history and a primary follow-up question, with a subset (6 entries) extending to a second-tier follow-up. These items evaluate a model’s capacity to support longitudinal clinical care such as medication adjustments, relapse monitoring, and continued patient engagement. All entries were curated from expert-reviewed psychiatric cases to reflect authentic post-treatment scenarios.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Classification:</span>
This component includes 418 psychiatric case descriptions, each annotated with both a broad diagnostic category and a specific disorder label. The classification task requires models to interpret narrative clinical data and assign appropriate labels by frameworks such as the DSM-5. Cases span multiple psychiatric disorders and are sourced from various clinical and educational texts. This task is crucial for evaluating a model’s ability to operationalize diagnostic taxonomy and differentiate between related disorders.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Management Plan:</span>
This dataset consists of 337 case-based question-answer pairs that test a model’s ability to generate psychiatric management plans. Each scenario includes a clinical context, often supplemented with mental and physical examination findings. The goal is to evaluate how models handle care coordination, safety planning, treatment adherence, and multidisciplinary strategies. Questions are sourced from authoritative psychiatric references and reflect decisions clinicians make beyond diagnosis or single-treatment choices.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">6.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Clinical Approach:</span>
This subset contains 56 expertly crafted scenarios aimed at evaluating a model’s ability to reason through complex psychiatric cases. The questions focus on the rationale behind clinical decisions such as ruling out differential diagnoses, identifying red flags, or determining the most appropriate investigative or management step. Each case includes multiple LLM-generated responses, enabling comparative analysis. The dataset emphasizes the "how" of clinical reasoning, offering a rigorous benchmark for evaluating the interpretive depth of psychiatric decision support systems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">7.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mental QA:</span>
This dataset includes 326 expert-annotated question-answer pairs that test psychiatric knowledge, definition, and conceptual understanding. Most questions 231 explicitly begin with "define," while others explore variations such as "what is," "differentiate," and "describe." Questions are sourced primarily from the Casefile and Case Vignettes series, and include items such as "How would you define thought withdrawal?" or "What are the key diagnostic clues that suggest this patient has histrionic personality disorder?" This dataset targets factual psychiatric knowledge and terminology aligned with DSM-5, ICD-10, and formal psychiatry training curricula.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i8" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">8.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequential Question Answering:</span>
This dataset comprises 32 clinical vignettes, each followed by a structured sequence of five diagnostic and management questions. The questions reflect a progressive clinical reasoning path, covering: (1) differential diagnosis, (2) supporting evidence and additional needed information, (3) etiological factors, (4) treatment options, and (5) prognosis. The dataset supports multi-turn reasoning and longitudinal clinical understanding. Each case is also tagged with a classification label (32 unique categories), including disorders like schizophrenia, psychotic depression, and substance-related disorders. This structure is ideal for evaluating retention, reasoning, and contextual depth in LLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i9" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">9.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multiple Choice Questions (MCQs):</span> 
This task group encompasses structured assessment formats commonly used in psychiatric exams. It is subdivided into four types of multiple-choice datasets:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I1.i9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1.i9.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MCQ</span>: 
<br class="ltx_break">1473 standard multiple-choice questions, each with 4-5 answer options. These are clinical knowledge checks grounded in patient histories and symptom-based reasoning. For instance, one item describes a 40-year-old male with choreiform movements and a family history of psychiatric illness, requiring identification of the gene location associated with Huntington’s disease.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Exams</span>: 
<br class="ltx_break">A curated exam-style dataset, designed to simulate standardized testing disorders. This set is split into two parts for evaluation: EMI 300 and MCQ 300. These items assess factual recall and decision-making across psychiatric domains.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EMI (Extended Matching Items)</span>: 
<br class="ltx_break">A clinical reasoning task where a thematic list of 8-15 options is presented, followed by multiple clinical vignettes requiring matched answers. The EMI format encourages fine-grained differentiation across closely related disorders or treatments. The dataset contains 277 whole EMI clusters, each with shared headers and grouped questions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EMI Separated</span>: 
<br class="ltx_break">To support fine-tuning and task-specific evaluation, the full EMI format was disaggregated into 1037 standalone question items. Each retains its original header and option list for context, but functions as an independent unit. This format simplifies scoring and improves input granularity during training.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i9.I1.i4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Although the original data includes answer explanations, these were excluded in modeling tasks to maintain fairness and prevent leakage.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Each task type is intended to test different facets of psychiatric competence, ranging from factual recall and concept definitions to complex clinical reasoning and longitudinal patient care. This diversity in task design allows for a comprehensive evaluation of both general-purpose and domain-specialized LLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 Datasets ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents a matrix of the dataset. Each row corresponds to a specific educational resource (book), and each column represents one of the eleven QA tasks. A checkmark (✓) indicates that the book contains content suitable for that task. For example, Case Files Psychiatry supports nearly all tasks, making it ideal for multi-task learning models. Conversely, MCQs in Psychology focus solely on multiple-choice questions, and the Casebook in Abnormal Psychology is annotated for its relevance to follow-up care and classification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This matrix provides a clear taxonomy of task-resource alignment, allowing for filtered dataset construction. Researchers can, for example, extract subsets specific to diagnostic reasoning or build MCQ-only datasets for exam simulation. The manual annotation process guarantees transparency, interpretability, and adaptability to various downstream applications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The curated nature of this dataset, grounded in expert-reviewed content, ensures its relevance to real-world psychiatric practice. It offers a robust and modular benchmark for evaluating LLMs in clinical decision support, medical training, and knowledge-grounded natural language processing. By incorporating diverse task types and detailed evaluation protocols, this dataset provides a comprehensive framework for measuring model performance and guiding improvements in mental health AI.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>The table shows which psychiatry textbooks and case-based resources include content related to specific clinical tasks. A check mark (✅) indicates coverage of a task, helping identify which resources contribute to each area in the dataset collection.
</figcaption>
