<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section outlines the experimental framework used to evaluate the performance of LLMs across a diverse set of psychiatric question-answering (QA) tasks. We describe the eleven task types designed to test specific aspects of psychiatric reasoning and detail the models employed, including both general-purpose LLMs and domain-specialized medical models. The evaluation methodology includes both automatic metrics and LLM-as-a-judge assessments to capture performance on accuracy, reasoning depth, and clinical alignment. By structuring our experiments around realistic clinical tasks and rigorous evaluation criteria, we aim to produce insights into the capabilities and limitations of current LLMs in mental health domains.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Task Types</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The task addressed in this work is a Question Answering (QA) problem in the medical domain, with a specific focus on psychiatry. The dataset consists of natural language questions and expert-curated answers derived from authoritative psychiatry textbooks. These questions span a variety of clinically relevant scenarios and concepts, reflecting the types of reasoning and decision-making tasks encountered in psychiatric practice and education.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike traditional QA systems focused on a single type of question, this dataset encompasses eleven distinct QA tasks, each representing a different cognitive or clinical function in psychiatry. These tasks range from diagnostic reasoning to treatment planning, classification, and sequential case-based analysis. The goal is to evaluate the ability of QA models to understand, reason, and generate medically accurate responses across these varied task types.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given the complexity and depth of psychiatric knowledge, this work aligns with applications in clinical decision support, medical education, and knowledge-grounded language modeling. The QA challenges posed by this dataset require both factual recall and higher-order reasoning consistent with medical standards such as the DSM-5 and ICD-10.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we briefly describe each of the eleven task types included in the dataset:</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1 </span>Task 1: Diagnoses </h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This task focuses on determining psychiatric diagnoses based on rich clinical vignettes that simulate real-world case presentations. Each entry in this section is a natural language question paragraph with a detailed patient scenario, often incorporating elements such as the patient’s psychiatric and medical history, presenting symptoms, and sometimes findings from the Mental State Examination (MSE) or contextual physical observations. The cases require models to identify either a likely diagnosis or generate a differential diagnosis, drawing on a nuanced understanding of symptom patterns, temporal progression, and psychosocial factors. For instance, cases may involve individuals with compulsive behaviors and intrusive thoughts indicative of obsessive-compulsive disorder or patients presenting with fear and avoidance consistent with specific phobias. The task challenges models to interpret and synthesize complex narratives, apply diagnostic reasoning, and align answers with formal criteria from standard psychiatric classifications such as the DSM-5 or ICD-10. As such, it plays a critical role in evaluating systems intended for clinical decision support, psychiatric triage, and medical education.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2 </span>Task 2:Treatment </h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This task centers on identifying appropriate treatment strategies for various psychiatric disorders based on the clinical scenarios provided. Each case includes a natural language question focused on management, along with contextual patient information such as symptoms, diagnosis, comorbidities, or previous treatment responses. The goal is to generate or select medically accurate treatment plans that align with current psychiatric guidelines. Treatment recommendations may involve pharmacological interventions such as prescribing antidepressants, antipsychotics, mood stabilizers, or anxiolytics, as well as non-pharmacological approaches, including cognitive behavioral therapy (CBT), psychoeducation, or hospitalization in severe cases. In some instances, the model must also account for patient-specific factors, such as age, substance use, risk level, or treatment resistance. This task tests the system’s ability to apply evidence-based psychiatry, reflect clinical guidelines (e.g., NICE, APA), and personalize care based on the patient’s profile. It is particularly relevant for clinical decision support systems, therapeutic recommendation engines, and educational tools aimed at medical trainees.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.3 </span>Task 3: Treatment Follow-Up</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This task addresses the longitudinal management of psychiatric patients, focused on follow-up care after an initial treatment decision has been made. This subset of clinical vignettes spans multiple time points, allowing models to reason about how a patient’s mental or physical disorders evolve in response to therapy. Each entry includes an initial clinical scenario, one or more follow-up reports (e.g., new symptoms, lab results, patient response), and a question prompting clinical judgment about how care should be adjusted. These follow-up scenarios often require interpreting medication effects, identifying emerging side effects (e.g., serotonin syndrome, orthostatic hypotension), assessing treatment adherence, or recommending new interventions. In addition to psychiatric symptoms, the task incorporates age-specific considerations (e.g., geriatric psychiatry) and physical health interactions, reflecting the complexity of real-world cases. Models are expected to make informed decisions about whether to continue, adjust, or discontinue treatment. This task simulates ongoing psychiatric care, testing a model’s ability to adapt recommendations over time and handle incomplete, evolving information, making it critical for clinical decision support, chronic care planning, and training in longitudinal psychiatric case management.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.4 </span>Task 4:Classification </h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A multi-label dataset for mental health disorder classification is created by manually extracting case studies and their
corresponding final classifications from multiple books. Each extraction undergoes a review process to ensure quality.
Disorder names across different books often show inconsistencies, with similar disorders labeled under varying names.
To standardize the terminology, two disorder lists are developed: a category disorders list and a specific disorders list.
Both lists are extracted from the DSM-5-TR and ICD-11 manuals and are reviewed by a licensed psychiatrist to ensure
accuracy. Disorder names from the books are then mapped to the standardized lists using Gemini 2.5 Pro. This mapping
process is conducted twice, once using the disorder categories list and once using the specific disorders list. As a result,
each case in the final dataset is assigned two sets of disorder labels: one set corresponding to the specific disorders and another set corresponding to their broader categories.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.5 </span>Task 5: Management Plan </h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This task involves generating a structured and contextually appropriate management plan for patients presenting with psychiatric symptoms. Each entry in this portion’s detailed clinical history is often accompanied by information from a Mental State Examination (MSE) and Physical Examination, followed by a question such as "How would you manage this case?" or "What interventions should you offer?". The answers outline holistic, step-by-step care strategies, integrating diagnostic clarification, risk assessment, therapeutic planning, and follow-up. Management plans may include pharmacological treatments (e.g., SSRIs for generalized anxiety), psychological therapies (e.g., CBT for OCD), psycho-education, social interventions, and referral pathways. The task requires models to interpret multiple layers of clinical input and propose management approaches aligned with psychiatric guidelines (e.g., NICE or APA). It emphasizes the practical application of knowledge in real-world clinical decision-making and supports training for complex care coordination in psychiatric settings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.6 </span>Task 6: Clinical Approach</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This task focuses on evaluating the appropriate clinical approach to a variety of psychiatric presentations, emphasizing step-by-step assessment and decision-making processes. Each question is tied to a case scenario and asks how clinicians should approach diagnosis, risk assessment, or management in a structured and evidence-based manner. Unlike tasks that ask for final diagnoses or treatments, this task emphasizes process-oriented reasoning on how clinicians gather information, rule out critical disorders, prioritize differential diagnoses, and choose appropriate initial interventions. The dataset features cases such as evaluating major depressive disorder, schizophrenia, panic disorder, or suicidal, with questions often framed as "What is the clinical approach to…?". Answers are grounded in established psychiatric practice guidelines and typically reference core elements such as history taking, Mental State Examination (MSE), collateral information, risk stratification, and initial management priorities. This task simulates the real-world cognitive workflow of psychiatric professionals, making it essential for models designed to assist with clinical decision-making, medical education, and case-based simulation training in psychiatry.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS7" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.7 </span>Task 7: Mental QA </h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This task involves the definition and clarification of core psychiatric concepts, syndromes, and clinical terms. Each question in the dataset prompts a concise and medically accurate explanation of a term or concept relevant to mental health, such as "chelonian," "bizarre delusions," or the pharmacological class "selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors." These terms are foundational to understanding clinical psychiatry and are often encountered in diagnostic interviews, treatment planning, and academic contexts. The answers are short, definitional statements drawn from clinical knowledge and psychiatric literature, consistent with sources like the DSM-5, pharmacological references, or foundational psychiatry textbooks. This task is designed to enhance conceptual grounding for QA models and promote explainability, particularly in systems intended for clinical education, patient-facing applications, or knowledge base construction. It also supports retrieval-augmented models and interpretable AI by encouraging explicit reasoning about medical terminology.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS8" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.8 </span>Task 8: Sequential Question Answering</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This task simulates dynamic, case-based psychiatric reasoning through a structured sequence of interrelated questions. Each scenario presents a detailed patient history, followed by a series of clinical queries that must be answered in context. The questions are designed to mirror the natural flow of psychiatric evaluation, moving from the initial differential diagnosis to evidence-based justification, etiological analysis, treatment planning, and finally prognostic estimation. For instance, a single case might begin by asking "What is the likely differential diagnosis?" followed by questions like "What information supports the diagnosis?", "What are the important etiological factors?", and "What treatment options are available?". This multi-turn format requires the model to maintain coherence, track evolving information, and apply lay clinical reasoning over time. It is beneficial for developing and evaluating QA systems that simulate real-world psychiatric interviews, medical board exam preparation, or decision-support tools in longitudinal patient care. This task emphasizes temporal context, cognitive continuity, and adaptive inference, making it a high-value benchmark for sequential understanding in medical AI.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS9" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.9 </span>Task 9: Multiple Choice Questions (MCQ)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This task includes three widely used assessment formats in psychiatric education and certification: (1) standard multiple-choice questions (MCQs), (2) extended matching items (EMIs), and (3) full-length exam simulations. Together, they are designed to evaluate knowledge recall, clinical reasoning, and decision-making in structured, test-like disorders.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.25in;">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multiple Choice Questions (MCQ):
The standard MCQs in our dataset are drawn from psychiatry-focused exams and cover a broad range of topics, including diagnosis, treatment, symptom classification, theoretical models, and psychopharmacology. Each question presents a clinical or conceptual prompt, followed by a list of five options, with one correct answer. These questions are designed to assess factual knowledge as well as applied understanding and clinical judgment.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extended Matching Items (EMI):
The EMI format groups several related questions under a single theme, such as diagnostic systems or therapeutic categories. Each item shares a standard answer list and must be matched with the most appropriate response. To maximize the utility of this format for QA evaluation, we split each EMI set into separate question-answer pairs while maintaining the original answer pool context. Enables fine-grained evaluation of the model’s ability to differentiate between similar options and interpret questions that depend on thematic nuance. The EMI task is particularly effective at testing classification accuracy, thematic grouping, and structured reasoning across semantically related content.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Exam Simulations:
We also included complete exam sets composed of multiple MCQs delivered together, replicating the structure and pacing of real psychiatric board exams. These exams are designed to simulate actual test disorders, including variation in difficulty, topic coverage, and question style. This component allows for evaluation of performance across a complete test and supports use cases such as automated exam preparation and curriculum-aligned feedback systems.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, these three formats provide a comprehensive benchmark for evaluating model performance on structured psychiatric assessments. They also support practical applications in educational settings, such as intelligent tutoring, exam readiness, and adaptive learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Language Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we detail the LLMs evaluated throughout our study, covering both general-purpose and domain-specialized systems. The goal is to assess their capabilities in handling various clinically relevant tasks, such as diagnosis, treatment planning, and psychiatric reasoning. By comparing general LLMs with medical-domain-specific models, we aim to highlight the performance trade-offs and advantages of each category. This dual perspective provides a clearer understanding of how LLMs can be optimized or selected for different healthcare applications.</p>
</div>
