</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure a well-rounded and insightful evaluation of LLMs in the context of psychiatric question answering, we selected a diverse group of medical-focused models that reflect a range of architectures, parameter scales, and accessibility levels. This curation was designed to balance frontier performance with practical considerations such as openness, reproducibility, and ease of deployment.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our model set includes high-capacity open-source systems like Llama3-OpenBioLLM-70B, Palmyra-Med-70B, and Llama-MedX v3.2, which leverage recent advances in foundational models and are optimized for biomedical and clinical tasks. We also included JSL-MedLlama-3-8B-v2.0, a smaller model aimed at providing strong performance while remaining lightweight and adaptable. These open-source models are readily accessible via platforms such as Hugging Face and are suitable for both academic research and fine-tuned clinical deployments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, we also evaluated Med-PaLM (v2), a proprietary model developed by Google DeepMind. While not open-source, Med-PaLM represents a high-performing benchmark for medical reasoning and is accessible through Google Cloud’s Vertex AI platform. Its inclusion provides an important comparison point for understanding the trade-offs between commercial-grade deployment and the flexibility of open systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By including both open and closed models of varying sizes, we aim to assess not only raw accuracy on psychiatric tasks but also broader factors such as infrastructure requirements, cost, adaptability, and transparency, key considerations for real-world clinical and educational use. A summary of these models, along with their sources and access points, is provided in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2.2 Medical Models ‣ 4.2 Language Models ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Evaluation Methodology</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the performance and alignment of model outputs, we employ a set of evaluation metrics designed to quantify agreement, consistency, and quality across different prompts and models. These metrics allow us to systematically compare outputs, identify patterns in model behavior, and measure improvements across prompt variations. In particular, we focus on agreement counts between model pairs to capture alignment and shared reasoning under different prompting disorders.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1 </span>Accuracy Metrics</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We mainly use two accuracy metrics in this work;</p>
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.25in;">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Balanced Accuracy</span>
(BA) is used to handle imbalanced datasets by computing the average recall for each class. It is defined as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="BA=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{TP}{TP+FN}+\frac{TN}{TN+FP}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">BA=\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{TP}{TP+FN}+\frac{TN}{TN+FP}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">BA helps in evaluating the model’s performance when the class distribution is imbalanced.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Accuracy</span> is the proportion of correctly classified samples among the total number of samples:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Accuracy=\frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Accuracy=\frac{TP+TN}{TP+TN+FP+FN}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Accuracy gives an overall measure of correctness, but it may not be reliable in cases of class imbalance, as it can be biased towards the majority class.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TP</annotation></semantics></math> (True Positives) and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FN</annotation></semantics></math> (False Negatives) are used to compute recall (sensitivity), while <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="TN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">TN</annotation></semantics></math> (True Negatives) and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="FP" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">FP</annotation></semantics></math> (False Positives) are used to compute specificity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2 </span>Extended Matching Item Evaluation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the EMI set, each question consists of multiple subquestions, all of which must be answered based on shared response options. To ensure accurate data evaluation, the dataset was assessed in two distinct formats: first, as a complete EMI set (full-format evaluation), and second, by treating each subquestion as an independent query with its evaluation (separated format).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the full EMI set format, scoring follows the Partial Scoring System (PCS), which assigns partial credit based on the proportion of correctly answered subquestions in a set. In contrast, the separated subquestion format evaluates each subquestion independently using a binary scoring system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For both formats, any instance of multiple answers being given for a single question is treated as incorrect and scored as zero.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">1. Partial Scoring System (PCS) - Full EMI Set Evaluation</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This evaluation method measures how well the model performs on grouped EMI questions, where each set consists of multiple related subquestions. The scoring system allows for partial credit, ensuring that models are not penalized excessively for a few incorrect subanswers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The score for the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th EMI set is computed as the proportion of correct subquestions:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EMI\_Score_{i}=\frac{1}{S_{i}}\sum_{j=1}^{S_{i}}c_{ij}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munderover><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EMI\_Score_{i}=\frac{1}{S_{i}}\sum_{j=1}^{S_{i}}c_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of EMI sets, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is Number of subquestions in the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th EMI set, and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{ij}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{ij}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>: Binary indicator for whether the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th subquestion in the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th set is answered correctly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The overall EMI accuracy is the average score across all sets:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="EMI\_Accuracy=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}EMI\_Score_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>_</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">EMI\_Accuracy=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}EMI\_Score_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">2. Individual Subquestion Evaluation - Separated EMI</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This approach evaluates each subquestion independently, allowing for standard binary scoring per subquestion. It offers a fine-grained view of model performance without being affected by the structure of the EMI sets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let:</p>
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>: Total number of individual subquestions across all EMI sets,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{k}\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{k}\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>: Binary indicator of correctness for the <math id="S4.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th subquestion when evaluated independently.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The overall accuracy is then computed as:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Subquestion\_Accuracy}=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{k=1}^{T}a_{k}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Subquestion_Accuracy</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Subquestion\_Accuracy}=\frac{1}{T}\sum_{k=1}^{T}a_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Answer Integrity Rule - Penalty for Multiple Answers</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In both evaluation methods, if the model produces multiple answers for a single question (whether set-based or individual), the answer is considered invalid and marked as incorrect. This ensures clarity, fairness, and aligns with human assessment standards.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{ij}=0\quad\text{if multiple answers are provided}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>if multiple answers are provided</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{ij}=0\quad\text{if multiple answers are provided}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Common Correct:</span>
This measures the number of questions answered correctly in both formats:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CommonCorrect}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbf{1}\left(F_{i}=1\land S_{i}=1\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CommonCorrect</mtext><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CommonCorrect}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbf{1}\left(F_{i}=1\land S_{i}=1\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are indicator variables for <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Full format</span> response and <span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Separated format</span> response on question <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=1</annotation></semantics></math> if the model answers question <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> correctly in the Full format and 0 otherwise. <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math> if the model answers question <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> correctly in the Separated format and 0 otherwise. <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\land" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∧</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\land</annotation></semantics></math>: The logical AND operator.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Common Incorrect:</span>
This measures the number of questions answered incorrectly in both formats:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{CommonIncorrect}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbf{1}\left(F_{i}=0\land S_{i}=0\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>CommonIncorrect</mtext><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{CommonIncorrect}=\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbf{1}\left(F_{i}=0\land S_{i}=0\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall Consistency:</span>
This score reflects how often the model yields the same result (correct or incorrect) across both formats:</p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Consistency}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbf{1}\left(F_{i}=S_{i}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Consistency</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Consistency}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbf{1}\left(F_{i}=S_{i}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Format Agreement Rate:</span>
This metric calculates the proportion of questions for which the model was correct in both formats, relative to all cases where the model was correct in at least one:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Agreement}=\frac{C}{C+O_{F}+O_{S}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Agreement</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Agreement}=\frac{C}{C+O_{F}+O_{S}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of the correct questions in both formats, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> are the correct questions only in Full format, and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> are the correct questions only in the separated format.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Format Divergence Rate:</span>
This captures how often the model’s correctness depends on format. A high divergence suggests inconsistent reasoning between question structures:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Divergence=\frac{O_{F}+O_{S}}{N}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>O</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></mrow><mi>N</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Divergence=\frac{O_{F}+O_{S}}{N}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.Px3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">These consistency metrics are crucial for evaluating the robustness and fairness of models, especially in educational and clinical settings where presentation format should not influence correctness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.3 </span>LLM as a judge</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantitatively evaluate the output quality of model-generated answers against ground truth references, we employed an automated approach leveraging an LLM as an expert judge <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>. Specifically, Llama 3 70B was configured for this evaluation task. The core objective was to compute a similarity score, ranging from 0 to 100, representing the degree of substantive congruence between the model-generated response (referred to as the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Candidate Answer</span>) and the reference ground truth (referred to as the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reference Answer</span>). Importantly, the evaluation was designed to focus on the core clinical content, including reasoning, correctness, and completeness of the response while disregarding differences in writing style or phrasing. <a href="#S10.SS3" title="10.3 Models Used for Evaluation Judging ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10.3</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recognizing the sensitivity of LLM evaluations to prompt design, we experimented with two distinct prompts, generated by tasking two capable LLMs, specifically GPT-4.5 and gemini-2.0-pro-exp-02-05, to create instructions for calculating the design similarity score (0–100). The prompt generation strategies differ: for GPT-4.5, we guided it toward a score-based prompt, employing a point-based system for different content components. Conversely, for gemini-2.0-pro-exp-02-05, we aimed for a more logic-driven prompt that relied on illustrative examples (few-shot learning) and emphasized understanding the overall structure and meaning of the Reference and Candidate Answers.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The prompt generated by GPT-4.5 is shown in Figure <a href="#S10.F15" title="Template 15 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, and the prompt generated by gemini-2.0-pro-exp-02-05 is shown in Figure <a href="#S10.F16" title="Template 16 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>. After comparative testing of both prompts with the Llama 3 70B judge, we selected the gemini-2.0-pro-exp-02-05-generated prompt for our final evaluation pipeline. This decision was driven by the observation that the logic-driven, example-based prompt elicited more consistent and reliable similarity scores across a diverse range of clinical tasks and model responses. These tasks included diagnostic assessment, treatment planning, follow-up care, management strategies, clinical approaches, mental health question-answering, and multi-turn clinical reasoning. The example-based prompt aligned more closely with our focus on nuanced content evaluation, as compared to the more rigid point-based system used in the GPT-4.5 prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Once individual similarity scores <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}\in[0,100]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0,100</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}\in[0,100]</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained for each task instance <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">final evaluation score</span> by taking the arithmetic mean across all evaluated instances:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Final Score}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}S_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Final Score</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Final Score}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of datasets used. This final score serves as a robust metric for quantifying the overall content-level alignment between model-generated outputs and expert-authored references across multiple clinical domains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.4 </span>Classification Evaluation</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since the classification task is multi-label in nature, each instance may be associated with multiple correct labels. To accurately assess model performance in this setting, we employed two complementary evaluation metrics: <span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Subset Accuracy</span> and <span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Weighted-Average F1-Score</span>. These metrics together offer a balanced view rewarding exact correctness while also accounting for partial matches and class imbalance. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite></p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Subset Accuracy (Exact Match Ratio)</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Subset Accuracy is a stringent metric that only considers a prediction correct if the predicted label set exactly matches the true label set no missing or extra labels are allowed. This makes it particularly valuable when precision in full-label prediction is critical. It answers the question: "How often did the model get everything exactly right?"</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Subset Accuracy}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbf{I}(\hat{Y}_{i}=Y_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Subset Accuracy</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Subset Accuracy}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\mathbf{I}(\hat{Y}_{i}=Y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Where: <math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is the total number of samples, <math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of true labels for the <math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th sample,
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of predicted labels,
<math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐈</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function, and equal to 1 if <math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}_{i}=Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}_{i}=Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and 0 otherwise.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Weighted-Average F1-Score (F1-weighted)</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While Subset Accuracy requires perfect prediction, the F1-weighted score provides a more flexible and balanced view of model performance. It evaluates how well the model balances <span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">precision</span> (avoiding false positives) and <span id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">recall</span> (avoiding false negatives) for each label independently. Then, it aggregates these per-label F1-scores into a single number using a weighted average, where more common labels contribute more to the final score.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, the F1-score for each label <math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> is calculated as the harmonic mean of precision (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>) and recall (<math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>):</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{1,l}=2\times\frac{P_{l}\times R_{l}}{P_{l}+R_{l}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{1,l}=2\times\frac{P_{l}\times R_{l}}{P_{l}+R_{l}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Then, the overall weighted-average F1-score across all labels <math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="S4.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{F1-weighted}=\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}}w_{l}\cdot F_{1,l}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>F1-weighted</mtext><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{F1-weighted}=\sum_{l\in\mathcal{L}}w_{l}\cdot F_{1,l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Where <math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all labels, and <math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> is the proportion of samples in which label <math id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> appears in the ground truth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS4.Px2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This metric ensures that performance on frequent labels contributes more heavily, helping to mitigate distortions caused by class imbalance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Parsing Model Outputs</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The process of interpreting and extracting meaningful information from LLM outputs emerged as a critical and non-trivial component of our methodology. While it may appear straightforward for tasks involving simple responses such as multiple-choice questions (MCQs) or numerical ratings the actual output behavior of large models introduced substantial complexity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Advanced reasoning-oriented models, such as DeepSeek Chat and Gemini Thinking, often diverged from strict output formats. Rather than outputting a clean label or value, they frequently embedded the answer within lengthy justifications or explanations. This behavior required parsing not just the final answer, but also understanding the structure of the response and retaining the embedded reasoning what we refer to as <span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"printing the reasoning"</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Consequently, our methodology included customized parsers and heuristics tailored to each model’s output style, allowing us to isolate the relevant information while preserving contextual evidence to support qualitative evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Methodology Implementation</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section outlines the comprehensive methodology used to develop, refine, and evaluate the PsychiatryBench dataset a benchmark designed to assess the clinical reasoning capabilities of LLMs in psychiatry. We describe the end-to-end pipeline, beginning with the manual curation of high-quality psychiatric question-answer (QA) pairs from evidence-based sources, followed by rigorous filtering and formatting procedures to ensure clinical accuracy and educational value. We then present the framework used to evaluate model responses across a diverse set of task types, including both structured and open-ended formats. Finally, we detail the design and implementation of specialized tasks that test distinct dimensions of psychiatric competence, such as classification, clinical reasoning, and treatment planning. This modular and scalable methodology provides a robust foundation for benchmarking LLM performance in real-world psychiatric assessment scenarios.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>PsychiatryBench Development Pipeline</h3>

<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09711v1/Work_flow_final.png" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/233;" width="476" height="233" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Workflow for the PsychiatryBench study: manual extraction and processing of clinical QA samples followed by LLM evaluation across eleven task types.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our methodology begins with systematic manual extraction of content from standard evidence-based psychiatry textbooks. Subject matter experts thoroughly review and select clinical material with high educational and diagnostic value, vibrant case studies, descriptive patient scenarios, and disorder-specific treatment information. From this material, a diverse set of question-answer (QA) pairs is constructed to represent real-world psychiatric reasoning. The QA items are crafted across eleven core task types: Diagnosis, Treatment, Mental QA, Sequential QA, Management Plan, Treatment Follow-up Clinical Approach, Classification, Multiple Choice Questions (MCQ), and Extended Matching Items (EMI). These task types were chosen for their ability to reflect lay clinical reasoning rather than rote knowledge recall.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Following construction, the QA items are passed through a rigorous filtering process designed to ensure clarity, clinical validity, and educational utility. Ambiguous, overly simple, or redundant items are removed, and the remaining questions are reviewed for content balance and diagnostic diversity. These filtered QA items form the foundation of the PsychiatryBench dataset. In this phase, questions are sorted by task type and formatted consistently, allowing for structured downstream evaluation. A comprehensive data cleaning step is performed next, standardizing answer styles, resolving typographic issues, and ensuring uniform metadata. The result is a refined, high-quality QA dataset that tests not only factual recall but also diagnostic reasoning, treatment planning, and sequential clinical decision-making in psychiatry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the performance of LLMs, we apply each model to the finalized PsychiatryBench dataset. Each model is prompted to generate answers across all eleven selected task types. Importantly, in addition to serving as QA generators, LLMs are also used as evaluators or judges, scoring the generated responses based on accuracy, completeness, and clinical relevance. This dual use of LLMs helps automate and scale the evaluation process while maintaining consistency across judgments. A structured rubric is applied to produce a final score for each model, capturing performance across various clinical dimensions. This evaluation framework enables a robust and scalable benchmark for assessing how well LLMs understand, reason through, and respond to complex psychiatric questions, grounded in authentic medical education content.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Filtering, Refinement, and Data Cleaning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following construction, the QA items are passed through a rigorous filtering process to ensure clarity, clinical validity, and educational utility. Ambiguous, overly simplistic, or redundant items are systematically removed. The remaining questions are reviewed to ensure a balance of content and a diversity of represented diagnoses. These filtered QA items form the foundation of the PsychiatryBench dataset. In this phase, questions are sorted by task type and formatted consistently to allow for structured downstream evaluation. A comprehensive data cleaning step is then performed, which involves standardizing answer styles, resolving typographic issues, and ensuring all metadata is uniform. The result is a refined, high-quality QA dataset that tests not only factual recall but also diagnostic reasoning, treatment planning, and sequential clinical decision-making in psychiatry.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Prompting Strategy</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All models were evaluated using a standardized zero-shot prompting approach. While the same prompt format was applied uniformly across all models to ensure fairness in comparison, prompt design was tailored to each specific task type within PsychiatryBench. For example, diagnostic reasoning tasks employed structured clinical case prompts, whereas factual knowledge tasks like Mental QA used direct definition queries. This task-specific prompting allowed us to preserve the integrity and intent of each evaluation domain while maintaining cross-model consistency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>LLM Models Implementation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implemented and evaluated a total of 11 LLMs, comprising both general-purpose frontier models (e.g., Gemini 2.5 Pro, Claude Sonnet 4, LLaMA 3.3, QWQ-32) and medical domain-specific models (e.g., MedGemma, OpenBioLLM). These models were tested across all eleven PsychiatryBench task types to assess their performance in real-world psychiatric reasoning tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each model was prompted using standardized engineering and evaluation templates tailored to the specific requirements of each task. Model outputs were evaluated using both automatic metrics (e.g., accuracy, F1 score) and an LLM-as-a-judge framework, which relied on a reference model to score responses based on semantic similarity and clinical relevance. This dual evaluation strategy ensured consistency, accuracy, and clinical alignment across diverse task types.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>LLM Performance Evaluation Framework</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the performance of LLMs, we apply each model to the finalized PsychiatryBench dataset. Each model is prompted to generate answers across all eleven selected task types. Our evaluation framework employs a multi-faceted approach tailored to the specific nature of each task. For open-ended reasoning tasks such as Diagnosis, Treatment, and Management Plan, we utilize an LLM-as-a-judge methodology. In this dual-role capacity, a designated LLM scores the generated responses from other models based on accuracy, completeness, and clinical relevance, providing a nuanced measure of semantic alignment. For structured tasks, we apply specific quantitative metrics. For instance, the Classification task is evaluated using Subset Accuracy and F1-scores to handle its multi-label nature. At the same time, multiple-choice and Extended Matching Items are scored using accuracy and custom partial scoring systems. This hybrid evaluation framework, combining qualitative LLM-based scoring with task-specific quantitative metrics, enables a robust and scalable benchmark for assessing how well LLMs understand, reason through, and respond to complex psychiatric questions.
To ensure consistency, all generated outputs were evaluated using a single evaluator model (LLaMA 3.3 70B) across all tasks. Each output and its reference were scored using a prompt-generated similarity scoring rubric. The evaluator model remained constant throughout to avoid bias introduced by evaluator variation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.6 </span>Task-Specific Implementations</h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To capture the diverse skills required for comprehensive psychiatric reasoning, we developed five specialized task implementations within the PsychiatryBench framework. Each task is designed to probe a distinct facet of clinical expertise from high-level categorization of disorders to stepwise diagnostic reasoning, from rapid knowledge recall in board-style questions to complex pattern recognition in extended matching formats. Below, we outline the goals, data sources, construction workflows, and evaluation criteria for each task type: Classification, Clinical Approach, Multiple Choice Questions (MCQ), and Extended Matching Items (EMI). This modular design enables targeted analysis of model strengths and weaknesses across different dimensions of psychiatric decision-making.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS6.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.6.1 </span>Classification Task</h4>

<div id="S5.SS6.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The classification component of the PsychiatryBench was developed to evaluate the ability of LLMs to categorize psychiatric disorders based on clinical descriptions, symptom clusters, and established diagnostic systems such as the DSM-5 and ICD-10. Rather than requiring exact diagnoses, this task involved structured prompts asking models to assign each case to appropriate diagnostic categories (e.g., mood disorders, psychotic disorders) and specific disorders (e.g., bipolar I disorder, schizophrenia).
Clinical case samples were manually extracted from authoritative psychiatric casebooks and annotated with preliminary disorder labels. In parallel, two reference lists were created from the DSM-5-TR: one for high-level diagnostic categories and another for specific disorders. A licensed psychiatrist validated both lists to ensure clinical accuracy and alignment with formal diagnostic criteria.
A mapping process was then applied to align each sample with both the validated category-level and disorder-level labels. This dual-labeling schema enabled multi-label classification and allowed models to be evaluated on their ability to reason through hierarchical diagnostic structures.
Although the classification dataset was ultimately excluded from the final model evaluation to maintain focus on more reasoning-intensive tasks, it played a critical role in shaping the design and validation of other task types, such as Diagnosis, Mental QA, and Management Plan. It also provided key insights into how LLMs interpret psychiatric terminology, differentiate between overlapping disorders, and apply structured diagnostic logic capabilities that are foundational to clinical psychiatric reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.6.2 </span>Clinical Approach Task</h4>

<div id="S5.SS6.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Clinical Approach task in the PsychiatryBench is designed to evaluate a model’s ability to emulate the diagnostic thought process of a psychiatrist. Unlike other tasks that target direct question-answering, this component focuses on assessing logical reasoning, hypothesis formulation, and sequential clinical interpretation based on patient case narratives.
The process begins with the creation of patient history scenarios, which are either extracted from psychiatric textbooks or crafted to simulate real-world clinical cases. These cases are then used to generate open-ended clinical reasoning questions. Question generation is performed using the Gemini 2.5 Pro model, which is prompted to formulate questions that require interpretive and multi-step reasoning.
Each generated question undergoes a manual validation step. Questions that are deemed clinically relevant and educationally meaningful proceed to the next phase; otherwise, they are revised or rewritten to ensure clinical quality.
Once validated, the finalized cases and questions are presented to multiple LLMs. Each model is tasked with generating structured, reasoned responses to the open-ended clinical prompts. These responses are not evaluated solely for factual correctness, but also for the quality of clinical logic, appropriateness of diagnostic pathways, and overall coherence of the suggested approach.
To explore the effect of prompting methodology, two parallel evaluation tracks were conducted. In the first, questions were generated using the Gemini model. In the second, the same patient cases were used without the question-generation step, allowing for a direct comparison of LLM performance with and without prompt engineering. Each model’s output was scored across criteria of reasoning depth, clinical relevance, and judgment quality, yielding a final score.
This task is essential in assessing how well LLMs can replicate the nuanced reasoning process of trained psychiatrists and highlights their potential role in clinical decision support systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.6.3 </span>Multiple Choice Question (MCQ) Task</h4>

<div id="S5.SS6.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Multiple Choice Questions (MCQ) component was developed as part of the broader PsychiatryBench dataset to reflect the structured, high-efficiency testing style commonly used in psychiatric board examinations and educational settings. These questions typically consist of a question stem followed by four to five answer options, with only one correct choice. MCQs are widely used to assess knowledge recall, clinical recognition, and treatment matching, making them a familiar tool for both learners and educators in psychiatry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The initial pool of MCQs was manually curated from reputable psychiatry resources, including standardized exam preparation materials, question banks, and textbook-based assessments. Many of these original items were accompanied by detailed explanations or rationales justifying why the correct answer was appropriate and why distractors were incorrect. While this information is pedagogically useful, it introduces bias when used in LLM evaluation settings, where models may memorize answer justifications or be influenced by phrasing patterns. To prevent this, a dedicated preprocessing step was implemented to remove all explanatory text from each MCQ. Ensures that only the question stem and multiple-choice options were retained, creating a clean, unbiased format suitable for model interaction and evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to removing explanations, all answers were standardized. Involved converting varying answer label formats (e.g., Roman numerals, complete sentences, abbreviated codes) into a consistent A/B/C/D labeling scheme. Correct answers were annotated in a separate metadata field to facilitate scoring and model training. Furthermore, ambiguous or duplicate questions were filtered out during this process to maintain quality and reduce redundancy. Although MCQs were excluded from the final model evaluation phase in favor of tasks requiring open-ended reasoning and multi-step logic, they were retained as part of the broader dataset for auxiliary evaluation and pretraining. The MCQ component also served as a structural foundation for the more complex Extended Matching Items (EMI) task, which builds on similar principles but with greater contextual complexity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.6.4 </span>Extended Matching Items (EMI) Task</h4>

<div id="S5.SS6.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Extended Matching Items (EMI) component was developed as part of the PsychiatryBench dataset to emulate a standard format used in clinical assessments, particularly within medical and psychiatric education. EMI questions are structured to assess deeper clinical reasoning by presenting a list of possible answer options (the theme), followed by multiple clinical scenarios that require selecting the most appropriate answer from that list.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.SSS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.SSS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each EMI question set typically consists of the following elements:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.SSS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A theme (classes of antidepressants, types of psychotherapies)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An option list (usually 8–10 related choices)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several clinical vignettes, each requiring the selection of the most suitable answer from the option list</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.SSS4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS6.SSS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This format is handy for testing pattern recognition, differential diagnosis, and treatment decision-making in psychiatry. For instance, an EMI set might include different patient cases presenting with depressive symptoms, and the task would be to select the most appropriate treatment or classification subtype for each case. In our methodology, EMI questions were manually created or adapted from authoritative psychiatry textbooks by expert reviewers. Ensures that each item was clinically relevant, unambiguous, and reflected real-world psychiatric decision-making. The EMI format requires the language models not only to recognize correct terminology but also to apply contextual reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Results &amp; Discussion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents the results of our experimental evaluation across multiple psychiatric QA tasks, using a range of LLMs assessed by expert-generated reference answers and LLM-as-a-judge scoring. We analyze performance trends across models and task types, comparing output quality under different prompt strategies and exploring the strengths and weaknesses of each model in tasks such as diagnosis, treatment planning, and follow-up care. Our discussion addresses both quantitative outcomes and qualitative patterns in reasoning, highlighting areas where LLMs demonstrate promising clinical utility and where challenges remain in achieving safe, interpretable, and consistent psychiatric decision support.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1 </span>Diagnosis</h3>

