<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We generated model outputs using the Engineering template <a href="#S10.F3" title="Template 3 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and the Evaluation template <a href="#S10.F4" title="Template 4 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> separately. As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 6.3 Treatment Follow-Up ‣ 6 Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, all eight models exhibited varied performance on generating treatment follow-up outputs, with average similarity scores ranging from (66.5 %) to (86.5 %). The Gemini 2.5 Pro Preview (03-25) led the group at (86.5 %), followed by QWQ-32 at (83.7 %) and the Gemini 2.5 Flash Preview (04-17, Thinking) at (82.6 %). The secondary Flash Preview (04-17) achieved (80.2 %), while Deepseek-chat and Deepseek-R1 produced (79.4 %) and (78.7 %), respectively. Llama 3.3 70B score (75.0 %), and the earliest variant, Gemini 2.0 Flash, trailed at (66.5 %). The substantial (20.0 %) spread between the top and bottom performers highlights that follow-up coherence poses a greater challenge than initial treatment or diagnosis tasks. Gemini 2.5 Pro Preview’s strong showing suggests its latest context-tracking optimizations significantly enhance continuity in multi-turn recommendations. QWQ-32’s robust (83.7%) score further underscores its ability to maintain patient context across successive exchanges. By contrast, Gemini 2.0 Flash’s steep drop to (66.5 %) indicates limitations in its reasoning architecture and narrower context window when managing follow-up prompts. The approximately (16.1 %) improvement from Gemini 2.0 Flash to the Gemini 2.5 Flash Preview (04-17, Thinking) illustrates the clear benefit of the "Thinking" inference mode for preserving treatment trajectories. Meanwhile, Deepseek-chat and Deepseek-R1, both below the (80.0% ) mark, demonstrate competence with basic follow-up queries but lack the advanced continuity afforded by newer model iterations. Llama 3.3 70B’s (75.0% ) result similarly suggests that even large foundational architectures may struggle with nuanced multi-turn follow-through without specialized enhancements.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.4 </span>Classification</h3>

<figure id="S6.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>Classification Results: Disorder categories and specific disorders classification dataset evaluation on the chosen models. The underlined bold values are the best score for the specific column.</figcaption>
<table id="S6.T8.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S6.T8.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S6.T8.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S6.T8.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">LLM</span></th>
