</figure>
<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We generate model outputs using the Engineering prompt template <a href="#S10.F6" title="Template 6 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and the Evaluation prompt template <a href="#S10.F7" title="Template 7 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> separately.As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ 6.5 Management Plan ‣ 6 Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>, all eight models produced high-quality management plans, with average similarity scores ranging from (78.7 %) to (84.4 %). This narrow spread of (5.7 %) points indicates that even the lowest-scoring model generates outputs that align reasonably well with expert-authored references, while the top performers exhibit only modest gains over the rest.DeepSeek-R1 achieved the highest score at (84.4%), marginally outperforming Gemini 2.5 Pro Preview (03-25) at (84.1 %) and DeepSeek-chat at (83.5 %). These leading models consistently synthesized comprehensive therapeutic recommendations, patient education points, and follow-up strategies that closely matched the reference plans. The strong performance of Gemini 2.5 Pro Preview underscores the impact of its recent architectural refinements on complex planning tasks. Mid-tier models QWQ-32 at (82.9 %), Gemini 2.5 Flash Preview (04-17, Thinking) at (82.4 %), and Llama 3.3 70B at (82.1 %) also deliver robust outputs, suggesting that both reasoning enhancements ("Thinking" mode) and broad contextual training contribute to solid plan generation. Gemini 2.5 Flash Preview (04-17) score (81.1 %), while Gemini 2.0 Flash lagged at (78.7 %), highlighting the clear benefit of iterative updates and instruction-tuning in newer model versions. Despite high similarity scores overall, careful human review remains essential to catch any subtle omissions or safety concerns before clinical use.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.6 </span>Clinical Approach</h3>

<div id="S6.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We generated model outputs using the Engineering prompt template <a href="#S10.F8" title="Template 8 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> and the Evaluation prompt template <a href="#S10.F9" title="Template 9 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> separately. As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ 6.6 Clinical Approach ‣ 6 Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> summarizes each model’s performance on the Clinical Approach task under two prompt formulations. Under the original prompt (Approach 1), average similarity scores range from (86.7 %) to (90.1 %), demonstrating that all models can reliably outline diagnostic frameworks and management plans when given a clear, familiar instruction. With the reformulated prompt (Approach 2), scores fall between (82.8 %) and (87.5 %), indicating a consistent sensitivity to prompt wording across architectures. Deepseek-R1 and Deepseek-chat start at the top of the leaderboard with (90.0 %) and (90.1 %) under Approach 1, respectively, but both experience moderate declines of (4.1 %) and (4.7 %) when switched to Approach 2. Llama 3.3 70B drops from (89.7 %) to (83.9 %) (- 5.8 %), while QWQ-32 falls from (89.2 %) to (82.8 %) (- 6.4 %), marking the largest sensitivity among the group. These results suggest that even high-capacity models can struggle to generalize reasoning steps if the prompt structure shifts.By contrast, Gemini 2.5 Pro Preview (03-25) exhibits the greatest robustness, declining only from (89.2 %) to (87.5 %) (- 1.7 %). Its Flash variants also remain relatively stable, with the "Thinking" mode decreasing by (2.1 %) (87.5 % → 85.4 %) and the standard Flash by (3.5 %) (88.6 % → 85.1 %). This relative consistency underscores the value of instructional tuning and specialized inference strategies in maintaining performance when prompt formulations vary.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T10" class="ltx_table">
