</figure>
<div id="S6.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We generated model outputs using the Engineering prompt template <a href="#S10.F11" title="Template 11 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> and the Evaluation prompt template<a href="#S10.F12" title="Template 12 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> separately.As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ 6.8 Sequential Question Answering ‣ 6 Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, all eight models exhibit strong performance on the Sequential Question Answering task under Prompt 1, with average similarity scores ranging from (81.4 %) to (96.9 %). Gemini 2.5 Pro Preview (03-25) stands out with an exceptional (96.9 %), demonstrating its superior ability to maintain context and deliver coherent, expert-aligned answers across multiple turns. Deepseek-R1 and Deepseek-chat follow closely at (89.0 %) and (88.9 %), respectively, indicating robust sequential reasoning capabilities.Mid-tier models including Gemini 2.5 Flash Preview (04-17, Thinking) at (89.7 %), Gemini 2.5 Flash Preview (04-17) at (89.2 %), and Gemini 2.0 Flash at (90.0 %) also deliver consistently high-quality outputs, showcasing effective context tracking and answer continuity. QWQ-32 achieves a solid (86.2 %), reinforcing its reliability in multi-turn clinical dialogues. These scores suggest that both "Thinking" inference modes and incremental flash updates materially enhance a model’s sequential QA performance. Llama 3.3 70B records the lowest similarity at (81.4 %), highlighting potential difficulties in capturing all nuanced dependencies between successive questions without additional prompt optimization. Nevertheless, its performance remains respectable, and prior analyses suggest that refined prompt Engineeringing could further close the gap. Overall, these results underscore that while top-tier proprietary models excel in sequential reasoning, prompt clarity and model updates play a critical role in elevating performance across the board.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.9 </span>Multiple Choice Questions (MCQ)</h3>

<div id="S6.SS9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents model performance on structured, objective assessment items commonly used in medical education. We start with single-best-answer questions (MCQs), followed by more complex matching and exam-style prompts. These formats test discrete factual recall, application of clinical reasoning, and the ability to integrate information across multiple elements.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS9.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.9.1 </span>Single-Answer Multiple-Choice Question</h4>

<div id="S6.SS9.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS9.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used the Engineering prompt template <a href="#S10.F13" title="Template 13 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> to generate the model outputs, and then evaluated them for accuracy in classification without using a separate evaluation prompt.As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ 6.9.1 Single-Answer Multiple-Choice Question ‣ 6.9 Multiple Choice Questions (MCQ) ‣ 6 Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>, model performance on the MCQ task varies considerably depending on prompt style. Under the baseline prompt (P1), accuracy ranges from (75.5 %) to (83.2 %) and balanced accuracy from (64.3 %) to (79.3 %). With the few-shot prompt (P2), overall accuracy spans (71.6 %) to (82.8 %) and balanced accuracy (63.7 %) to (74.4 %). This indicates that while some models maintain consistency across both formats, others particularly those sensitive to prompt examples experience notable drops.Gemini 2.5 Pro Preview (03-25) delivers the most robust performance overall, scoring (81.7 %)/(79.3 %) in P1 accuracy/BA and improving to (82.8 %)/(74.4 %) in P2. Deepseek-R1 achieves the highest P1 accuracy at (83.2 %) but falls to (71.6 %) under P2, suggesting a strong grasp of standalone questions but shows better generalization when provided with examples. Llama 3.3 70B and QWQ-32 show stable results around (77.1 %)/(72.2 %) and (78.4 %)/(74.8 %) in P1, and (77.5 %)/(72.3 %) and (76.8 %)/(71.5 %) in P2 highlighting reliable reasoning unaffected by prompt format.By contrast, Gemini 2.0 Flash underperforms relative to its successors, with balanced accuracy at (64.3 %)/(65.2 %) in P1/P2 despite moderate accuracy (75.59 %)/(75.2 %). The Gemini 2.5 Flash Preview variants also show mixed results: the "Thinking" mode drops to (74.1 %)/(63.7 %) in P2, while the standard Flash holds (79.1 %)/(71.5 %). These findings underscore that MCQs though simpler than open-ended tasks still reveal differences in how models leverage prompt contexts and example-based cues to select correct answers.</p>
</div>
