<td id="S6.T13.2.10.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">79.1</td>
<td id="S6.T13.2.10.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">71.5</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS9.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">6.9.2 </span>Extended Matching Item</h4>

<div id="S6.SS9.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS9.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used the Engineering prompt template <a href="#S10.F13" title="Template 13 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> to generate the model outputs, and then evaluated them for accuracy in classification without using a separate evaluation prompt. As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ 6.9.2 Extended Matching Item ‣ 6.9 Multiple Choice Questions (MCQ) ‣ 6 Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, all eight models achieve strong performance on the EMI task, with average similarity scores ranging from (72.2 %) to (87.94 %). Gemini 2.5 Pro Preview (03-25) leads the field under both prompts scoring (87.63 %) on Prompt 1 and (87.9 %) on Prompt 2 demonstrating exceptional consistency when matching complex, multi-option stems. Deepseek-R1 and Gemini 2.5 Flash Preview (04-17, Thinking) follow closely, both posting above (85.4 %) across disorderss, which underscores their robust reasoning capabilities even when instructions shift.In the mid tier, QWQ-32 records (78.5 %)/(79.8 %) under Prompts 1/2, while Deepseek-chat posts (80.34 %)/(79.8 %). These models handle extended contexts competently but display modest sensitivity to prompt wording, with score fluctuations under (1.1 %) points. Gemini 2.5 Flash Preview (04-17) shows the greatest prompt responsiveness, improving from (79.4 %) to (84.5 %) illustrating how optimized phrasing can yield substantial gains even on intricate matching tasks. At the lower end, both Gemini 2.0 Flash and Llama 3.3 70B trail their peers. Gemini 2.0 Flash drops from (80.1 %) on Prompt 1 to (75.5 %) on Prompt 2 a decline of (4.61 %) while Llama 3.3 70B remains flat at (72.3 %)/(72.2 %), indicating limited adaptation to the EMI format and instruction changes. These results highlight that, although modern LLMs can approach expert-level performance on EMIs, achieving maximum reliability requires careful prompt design and potentially EMI-specific instruction tuning. We also compared performance on the full-cluster versus separated-item EMI formats.
As shown in Table <a href="#S10.T19" title="Table 19 ‣ 10.2 Extended Matching Item ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>, the full EMI format consistently outperforms the separated format, with average similarity scores of (84.1 %) versus (81.4 % )across all eight models. This finding further underscores the importance of preserving holistic context when matching complex, multi-option stems. The comparison between the full EMI format and the separated format is presented in section 10.2 in the appendix, where the full EMI format consistently outperforms the separated format across all eight models.</p>
</div>
