</figure>
<div id="S6.SS9.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS9.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used the Engineering prompt template <a href="#S10.F13" title="Template 13 ‣ 10.4 Prompt Templates ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> to generate the model outputs, and then evaluated them for accuracy in classification without using a separate evaluation prompt.As shown in Table <a href="#S6.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ 6.9.3 Exams ‣ 6.9 Multiple Choice Questions (MCQ) ‣ 6 Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, model performance on the Exams task under Prompt 1 ranges from (66.5 %) to (80.4 %), while under Prompt 2 it spans (65.7 %) to (76.8 %). Gemini 2.5 Pro Preview (03-25) leads the cohort on Prompt 1 with (80.4 %), reflecting its strong ability to comprehend and synthesize multi-sentence clinical vignettes into expert-aligned answers. However, its drop to (71.3 %) under Prompt 2 highlights that its exam-style reasoning is sensitive to instruction phrasing. Deepseek-R1 posts the next highest scores at (79.2 %)/(74.3 %), indicating reliable baseline performance and moderate resilience to prompt reformulation. Gemini 2.5 Flash Preview (04-17, Thinking) variant demonstrates the greatest robustness, scoring (78.4 %) on Prompt 1 and maintaining a high (76.8 %) on Prompt 2 a decline of only (1.68 %) suggesting that its enhanced reasoning mechanisms effectively preserve context and structure even when instructions change. Mid-tier models such as QWQ-32 ((73.4 %)/(67.2 %)), Gemini 2.5 Flash Preview (04-17) at ((73.1 %)/(71.6 %)), and Deepseek-chat ((71.4 %)/(69.2 %)) show competent understanding of exam questions but experience steeper drops of (5-6 %) under the alternate prompt, implying they rely more heavily on specific cue wording.At the lower end, Gemini 2.0 Flash and Llama 3.3 70B struggle the most: Gemini 2.0 Flash edges up slightly from (69.9 %) to (71.1 %) when given the second prompt perhaps benefiting from clearer examples while Llama 3.3 70B remains nearly flat at (66.5 %)/(65.7 %). These results suggest that without targeted prompt Engineeringing or exam-specific tuning, foundational models may underperform on complex, structured assessment items. Overall, the data underscore that both advanced model architectures and prompt clarity are critical for reliable automated evaluation of professional-level exam questions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
