<td id="S6.T16.2.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">MCQ</td>
<td id="S6.T16.2.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">87.4</td>
<td id="S6.T16.2.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">81.6</td>
<td id="S6.T16.2.11.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">74.2</td>
<td id="S6.T16.2.11.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">(87.4%) MedGemma</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T16.2.12" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T16.2.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Exam</td>
<td id="S6.T16.2.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">69.2</td>
<td id="S6.T16.2.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">77.0</td>
<td id="S6.T16.2.12.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">65.3</td>
<td id="S6.T16.2.12.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">(80.4%) Gemini 2.5 Pro</td></tr>
<tr id="S6.T16.2.13" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S6.T16.2.13.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">EMI</td>
<td id="S6.T16.2.13.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">72.5</td>
<td id="S6.T16.2.13.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">83.9</td>
<td id="S6.T16.2.13.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">73.1</td>
<td id="S6.T16.2.13.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">(87.9%) Gemini 2.5 Pro</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T16" title="Table 16 ‣ 6.10 Benchmarking LLMs Against Top Performers ‣ 6 Results &amp; Discussion ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> summarizes the performance of four models, including MedGemma, Sonnet 4, QWEN 3 32B, and Gemini 2.5 Pro across eleven clinical and mental-health tasks. Overall, Sonnet 4 and Gemini 2.5 Pro achieve the highest scores on the majority of tasks. Sonnet 4 leads on Treatment (84.5 %), Management Plan (84.8 %), and Mental QA (89.35 %), while Gemini 2.5 Pro outperforms on Diagnoses (86 %), Treatment Follow-Up (86.5 %), Sequential QA (96.5 %), Exam (80.4 %), and EMI (87.9 %). Deepseek-chat narrowly tops Clinical Approach at 90.01 %, essentially tying with the others at 90 %. DeepSeek R1 demonstrates strength in broad Classification (Disorder Categories) (A = 0.54, F1 = 0.72), and on Specific Disorders Classification (A = 0.44, F1 = 0.50).No single model dominates across all tasks. Gemini 2.5 Pro excels in sequential reasoning and recall-intensive tasks such as Sequential QA and Exam, likely due to its larger parameter count and advanced contextual understanding. QWEN 3 32B strong performance in treatment-related tasks and mental QA suggests effective fine-tuning on healthcare dialogues. MedGemma’s superior classification of specific disorders indicates strength in entity recognition and fine-grained labeling, while QWEN 3 32B’s relative success on broader disorder categorization may reflect robust generalization.The modest F1 scores in classification tasks highlight an area for improvement: models struggle with balanced precision and recall when differentiating nuanced categories. Future work should explore ensemble approaches or targeted fine-tuning on underrepresented classes to boost classification F1. Additionally, incorporating external medical knowledge bases may further enhance diagnostic and treatment reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS11" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.11 </span>Comparative Performance Radar of All Models</h3>

<div id="S6.SS11.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This radar chart presents the comparative performance of 12 LLMs across eleven psychiatric reasoning tasks from the PsychiatryBench. Each axis corresponds to a distinct task category, including Diagnosis, Treatment, Treatment Follow-Up, Classification (Categories and Specific Disorders), Management Plan, Clinical Approach, Mental QA, Sequential QA, MCQ, and EMI. The scores reflect similarity-based evaluation metrics computed using an LLM-as-a-judge method with Llama 3 70B as the evaluator. Notably, Gemini 2.5 Pro, Gemini 2.5 Flash Thinking, and QWQ-32 consistently perform near the top across most domains, particularly in Diagnosis, Sequential QA, and EMI, while Deepseek R1 and Qwen 3 32 show relatively lower performance in Classification tasks. The pronounced dip in scores for "Classification (Specific Disorders)" highlights a consistent challenge across all models. This visualization underscores the strengths and limitations of each model across complex psychiatric tasks, providing insights into areas requiring further model refinement.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.09711v1/radar.png" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/225;" width="476" height="225" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Radar chart showing LLM performance across 11 psychiatric tasks in the PsychiatryBench. Models like Gemini 2.5 Pro and QWQ-32 lead overall, while classification tasks remain the most challenging.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Limitations and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although this study offers valuable information on the capabilities of LLMs for mental health tasks, it is important to recognize several limitations that may affect the interpretation and generalizability of our findings. In addition to the future directions that can help achieve higher performance.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Limitations</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While PsychiatryBench represents a significant step towards the clinically grounded evaluation of LLMs in psychiatry, its development and scope were shaped by several inherent limitations. These constraints, which our work seeks to begin addressing, stem primarily from the nature of the available source material and the practicalities of evaluation at scale.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The foremost limitation is a direct consequence of the critical research gap we identify: the absence of large-scale, publicly available, and expert-validated clinical datasets for mental health. Forced a reliance on meticulous <span id="S7.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">manual curation</span> from authoritative textbooks. While this process ensures unparalleled clinical validity, the core contribution of our work is inherently labor-intensive. As a result, the scale of PsychiatryBench, though substantial, is smaller than benchmarks built on synthetic data or automated web-scraping. It was a deliberate trade-off to prioritize clinical safety over sheer data volume.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, our reliance on published textbooks introduces a trade-off between authoritative knowledge and real-world clinical practice. The vignettes in these resources are, by design, well-structured and pedagogically focused to illustrate specific diagnostic principles. They may not fully capture the ambiguity, noise, and unstructured nature of raw clinical data, such as real-time patient dialogues or fragmented electronic health record (EHR) notes. Consequently, while PsychiatryBench robustly tests an LLM’s reasoning against expert-curated knowledge, performance may not perfectly generalize to the complexities of live, unfiltered clinical interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A related point is that the distribution of psychiatric disorders within PsychiatryBench does not mirror their real-world epidemiological prevalence. It is a direct consequence of our source materials; psychiatric textbooks often over-represent rare, complex, or "classic" cases to serve specific pedagogical goals. Therefore, models evaluated on PsychiatryBench are tested on their ability to handle a teaching-oriented distribution of cases, which differs from the case mix they would encounter in a general clinical setting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, our evaluation methodology for open-ended tasks relies on LLM-as-a-judge. While this approach offers the scalability and consistency required to evaluate thousands of responses across numerous models, it is not a perfect substitute for human expert evaluation. LLM judges can have inherent biases and may not capture the full clinical nuance apparent to a practicing psychiatrist. We mitigated this by grounding the evaluation in expert-validated reference answers, meaning the judge’s task was one of comparing an output to a "gold standard" rather than making a de novo clinical judgment. However, a full-scale validation by a panel of human psychiatrists was beyond the scope of this work and remains a crucial future direction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">These limitations highlight the very challenges our work was designed to confront. The constraints on scale, data distribution, and evaluation methods are direct consequences of the field’s lack of high-fidelity psychiatric benchmarks. By navigating these issues and creating a transparent, clinically-grounded resource, PsychiatryBench establishes an essential baseline. It serves not only as an evaluation tool but as a foundational scaffold upon which future research can build to address these very limitations, paving the way for safer and more reliable AI in mental healthcare.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Future Work</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis identifies seven key priorities for advancing LLM applications in mental health, summarized in the following points:</p>
<ul id="S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.25in;">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expanded Dataset Coverage:</span> Future research should aim to include a wider range of psychiatric disorders, cultural contexts, and multimodal data sources (e.g., EHRs, imaging, and audio transcripts) to create a more representative benchmark. Collaborations with international psychiatric organizations could facilitate access to diverse clinical data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Automated and Scalable Annotation:</span> Developing robust automated tools for case extraction and annotation, validated by expert psychiatrists, would enable larger-scale datasets while maintaining clinical accuracy. Techniques like natural language processing (NLP) for structured data extraction could streamline this process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multimodal Evaluation Frameworks:</span> Integrating multimodal inputs, such as lab results or patient audio, into evaluation datasets would better simulate real-world clinical decision-making. Could involve adapting LLMs to process structured data alongside text or developing hybrid models for multimodal reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Broader LLM Comparisons:</span> Evaluating a diverse set of LLMs, including open-source models and those fine-tuned for medical domains, would provide deeper insights into their relative strengths and weaknesses in psychiatric applications.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Enhanced Evaluation Metrics:</span> Future work should focus on designing metrics that prioritize clinical safety, cultural sensitivity, and robustness against hallucinations. Incorporating human-in-the-loop evaluation by practicing psychiatrists could ensure alignment with clinical standards.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Comprehensive Task Diversity:</span> Expanding the task set to include empathetic dialogue, crisis intervention, and longitudinal care planning would enable a more complete assessment of LLMs’ potential in mental healthcare. Could involve creating conversational benchmarks like MentalChat 16K but grounded in expert-validated data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prompt Optimization Frameworks:</span> Systematic exploration of the prompt space, using automated prompt engineering tools, could identify optimal prompt structures for clinical tasks. Standardizing prompt formats across studies would improve reproducibility and comparability of results.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we have introduced PsychiatryBench, the first comprehensive, clinically grounded benchmark for evaluating LLMs in psychiatric applications. By curating over 5,300 question-answer pairs from eleven authoritative textbooks and casebooks, ranging from diagnostic vignettes and treatment planning to longitudinal follow-up and complex sequential reasoning, we ensure that model performance is measured against expert-validated, real-world clinical knowledge rather than unverified or synthetic data. Our eleven distinct task types probe the full spectrum of psychiatric reasoning, from factual recall and classification to multi-turn decision-making and management planning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Through rigorous evaluation of both general-purpose and medical-specialized LLMs, including proprietary systems such as Gemini 2.5 Pro and MedGemma, as well as open-source models like LLaMA and Palmyra-Med, we identify clear performance leaders and persistent gaps. Frontier models achieve strong alignment on single-turn tasks such as diagnosis and management planning (up to 8̃7 % similarity). However, performance degrades on multi-step follow-up, classification of comorbid disorders, and prompt robustness across varying formats. Our novel similarity scoring, along with consistency metrics for full versus separated EMIs and multi-label classification, provides a nuanced picture of models’ strengths and limitations in high-stakes clinical assessments.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These findings underscore both the promise and the perils of deploying LLMs in mental healthcare. On one hand, leading models demonstrate impressive capacity for clinical reasoning, empathetic response, and knowledge synthesis; on the other, the risk of hallucinations, prompt sensitivity, and difficulties with multi-label, longitudinal scenarios highlight the necessity of human oversight and continued refinement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S8.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Looking ahead, PsychiatryBench offers a modular framework for future research. Extending the dataset with additional case complexities, such as cross-cultural presentations, pediatric and forensic psychiatry, and integrating multimodal inputs (e.g., speech, imaging) will further bridge the gap between AI evaluation and real-world clinical practice. Moreover, fine-tuning and retrieval-augmented approaches hold promise for improving model robustness and safety. We encourage the community to leverage PsychiatryBench as both a diagnostic tool to benchmark new models and as a scaffolding for developing clinically reliable, interpretable AI systems that can augment psychiatric care while rigorously safeguarding patient well-being.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Ethical Statement, Copyright, and Fair Use Statement</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The PsychiatryBench dataset was developed using content manually curated from publicly available, expert-authored psychiatric textbooks and casebooks. All material was selected and annotated by subject matter experts with a clinical background to support research in safe and effective psychiatric reasoning by AI models. No personal, identifiable, or patient-derived data were used in the construction of this dataset. The dataset does not contain any clinical notes, transcripts, or sensitive medical information from real individuals.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This research adheres to ethical guidelines regarding the use of educational content for research purposes. The dataset is intended solely for non-commercial research, academic, and educational uses that aim to improve the safety, reliability, and clinical alignment of language models in mental health applications. All references to psychiatric diagnoses, treatments, and case scenarios are drawn from simulated or publicly available educational materials, not real patient interactions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All content in the PsychiatryBench dataset is derived from publicly available and widely used psychiatric educational resources for academic research and benchmarking. The inclusion of questions and scenarios from books such as DSM-5-TR Clinical Cases, Stahl’s Psychopharmacology, and others is done under the principle of fair use, specifically for:</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S9.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:0.25in;">
<li id="S9.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Transformative use: The material has been substantially modified, structured, and reformatted into a new dataset format for the distinct purpose of evaluating AI systems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Non-commercial research: The dataset is intended solely for research and academic purposes, without any commercial exploitation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Limited scope and effect on market: Only selected, representative samples were used, not the entirety of any single source, and use of the dataset does not substitute for or compete with the original textbooks.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S9.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Users of the dataset are responsible for ensuring compliance with local copyright laws. They should obtain appropriate permissions if intending to reuse substantial parts of the original material for commercial or distribution purposes.</p>
</div>
</section>
