<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10 </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="S10.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.1 </span>Evaluation of medical LLMs</h3>

<div id="S10.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Table <a href="#S10.T17" title="Table 17 ‣ 10.1 Evaluation of medical LLMs ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>, we report the performance of six out-of-the-box medical LLMs on two clinical benchmarks: "Management Plan" and "Mental QA" (a diagnosis-oriented question-answering task). II-Medical-8B, an 8 billion-parameter model trained on clinical data, attains (76.81 %) on Management Plan and (76.84%) on Mental QA. OpenBio-LLM tuned for broad biomedical text scores (75.10 %) and (82.21 %), respectively. Med_palmyra, fine-tuned on narrative case reports, achieves (78.29 %) on Management Plan and (83.20% ) on Mental QA. We also evaluated JSL-MedLlama-3-8B-v2.0, but its results fell below (75 %) on both tasks, so it was not consider further; similarly, Med-PaLM (v2) was excluded due to its proprietary licensing and lack of local fine-tuning capability. Finally, MedGemma leads the field with (81.78 %) on the Management Plan and (87.11 %) on Mental QA. Owing to its superior accuracy and open-source license, we selected MedGemma for all subsequent experiments. Unlike proprietary offerings such as Med-PaLM which require access via restricted Google Cloud APIs, open-source models like MedGemma and OpenBio-LLM can be downloaded, run offline, and seamlessly integrated into custom evaluation pipelines, thereby promoting independent research and reproducibility.</p>
</div>
