<td id="S10.T19.2.8.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right"><span id="S10.T19.2.8.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_framed ltx_framed_underline" style="--ltx-border-color:#000000;">1.00</span></td>
<td id="S10.T19.2.8.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">0.00</td></tr>
<tr id="S10.T19.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S10.T19.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb">Flash 2.5 Thinking</th>
<td id="S10.T19.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">63.18</td>
<td id="S10.T19.2.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">86.12</td>
<td id="S10.T19.2.9.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S10.T19.2.9.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_framed ltx_framed_underline" style="--ltx-border-color:#000000;">86.52</span></td>
<td id="S10.T19.2.9.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb"><span id="S10.T19.2.9.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_framed ltx_framed_underline" style="--ltx-border-color:#000000;">86.2</span></td>
<td id="S10.T19.2.9.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">0.96</td>
<td id="S10.T19.2.9.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">0.96</td>
<td id="S10.T19.2.9.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">0.04</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S10.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S10.T18" title="Table 18 ‣ 10.2 Extended Matching Item ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> presents the raw counts of correct and incorrect responses for Prompt one across eight LLM models, both in the "full" (combined) and "separated" scoring disorders. Pro 2.5 achieved the highest number of entirely correct responses (953), followed by R1 (938) and Flash 2.5 Thinking (930). In the separated disorders, Flash 2.5 Thinking led with 888 correct items, followed by R1 (848) and QwQ (774). Conversely, deep seek chat had the fewest entirely correct (838) and separated correct (694) responses. Across models, the number of items incorrect in both disorders ranged from 98 (R1) to 198 (deep seek chat), indicating variability in robust failures. We will compare the best EMI model with the best MCQ model. This dataset was converted from EMI to MCQ when separated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S10.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S10.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Total Correct (Full): Pro 2.5 (953) &gt; R1 (938) &gt; Flash 2.5 Thinking (930)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Total Correct (Separated): Flash 2.5 Thinking (888) &gt; R1 (848) &gt; QwQ and Gemini Flash (both 799/774)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S10.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Incorrect in Both disorderss: R1 (98) showed the fewest dual-disorders errors, whereas deep seek chat (198) exhibited the most.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S10.T19" title="Table 19 ‣ 10.2 Extended Matching Item ‣ 10 Appendix ‣ Psychiatry-Bench: A Multi-Task Benchmark for LLMs in Psychiatry" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> summarizes performance in terms of accuracy, average partial scores, and format consistency. Pro 2.5 again achieved the highest full-accuracy (68.10 %) and the highest average partial score in the full disorders (87.94 %). Flash 2.5 Thinking attained the highest separated-accuracy (86.52 %) and the highest average partial score in the separated disorders (86.20 %). Flash 2.5 demonstrated perfect format consistency (1.00), with zero format divergence, while Pro 2.5 and deep seek chat exhibited the highest divergence rates (0.15 and 0.14, respectively).</p>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S10.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S10.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S10.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Accuracy (Full): Pro 2.5 (68.10 %) &gt; R1 (63.70 %) &gt; Flash 2.5 (61.73 %)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S10.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Accuracy (Separated): Flash 2.5 Thinking (86.52 %) &gt; R1 (81.85 %) &gt; Flash 2.5 (81.21 %)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S10.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S10.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Overall Consistency: Flash 2.5 (1.00) and Flash 2.5 Thinking (0.96) maintained the highest consistency between response format and expected output.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S10.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these results indicate that Pro 2.5 and Flash 2.5 Thinking lead in raw correctness and accuracy metrics, while Flash 2.5 variants excel in maintaining consistent formatting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">10.3 </span>Models Used for Evaluation Judging</h3>

<div id="S10.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our evaluation, the objective was to determine which prompt formulation leads to the highest level of agreement among different language models. The underlying assumption is that a more general and neutral prompt would guide models to produce more aligned outputs, reflecting an interpretation of the input and task requirements.
To quantify this, we examined the number of instances where two models produced the same output (i.e., agreements). The higher the agreement count between any two models, the more likely it is that the prompt encouraged uniform behavior and interpretation across model architectures.
The results are summarized across three prompts: P1, P2 (FS), and P3 (GPT-4.5), and across four model pairings: LLaMA &amp; GPT, GPT &amp; DeepSeek, DeepSeek &amp; LearnLM, LLaMA &amp; DeepSeek, GPT &amp; LearnLM, and LLaMA &amp; LearnLM.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S10.T20" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 20: </span>Model agreement counts across three prompt types: P1 (basic), P2 (few-shot), and P3 (GPT-4.5-tuned). Each value indicates the number of agreeing samples (e.g., "0 of 66"). Higher values suggest greater alignment in model outputs.</figcaption>
